WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.992 --> 00:00:14.445
So here we are.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.524
I'm at home, as I'm sure
many of you are, too.

00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:19.633
And we've all begun to understand

00:00:19.657 --> 00:00:21.712
how our relationship with ourselves,

00:00:21.736 --> 00:00:22.903
with each other

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:24.224
and the spaces we exist in

00:00:24.248 --> 00:00:27.669
can deeply impact
our sense of identity and purpose.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:30.542
So much has dramatically changed.

00:00:30.566 --> 00:00:33.936
There's a sense of distance now
unlike ever before.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.516
But what if I told you

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.904
that you could find a way
from your heart to your hand

00:00:39.928 --> 00:00:41.829
to reconnect again,

00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:45.861
and that through this practice
and embracing this cause,

00:00:45.885 --> 00:00:48.879
I could help you to recalibrate your mind

00:00:48.903 --> 00:00:52.379
so that you could explore
this new reality with joy,

00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:56.026
enthusiasm, imagination and hope?

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.804
And all it would take is a simple pen.

00:00:59.582 --> 00:01:00.916
To get you there,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.709
let's go back to the beginning.

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:06.403
As a kid growing up in a council estate
in Southeast London,

00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:07.982
I was an outsider.

00:01:08.006 --> 00:01:09.816
I'm the oldest of six kids,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.998
and all of my siblings look very English:

00:01:12.022 --> 00:01:14.553
blond hair, blue eyes, very cute.

00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:15.777
And then there was me:

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:18.984
half Nigerian, brown, with an Afro.

00:01:19.339 --> 00:01:22.077
So what happens when you look different

00:01:22.101 --> 00:01:23.514
and you feel different,

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:25.709
and in many ways,
start to think differently

00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:28.966
from everyone and everything around you?

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:34.354
How do you find your way
out of a dark, racist, homophobic

00:01:34.378 --> 00:01:36.331
and very lonely place?

00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:38.423
This is where the pen comes in.

00:01:38.836 --> 00:01:40.450
I started to draw.

00:01:40.474 --> 00:01:42.545
So as you can see, I've got this pen,

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:44.180
and it knows where it's going.

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:46.799
And I've learned very well
how to follow it.

00:01:46.823 --> 00:01:50.125
And the first thing I did is
I followed this line,

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:52.899
and I drew myself out of a culture

00:01:52.923 --> 00:01:55.603
that was only telling me
what I couldn't do.

00:01:55.944 --> 00:01:57.804
I trusted my pen,

00:01:57.828 --> 00:02:00.569
and it led me to Central Saint Martin's,

00:02:00.593 --> 00:02:02.815
a very fancy art school in London,

00:02:02.839 --> 00:02:05.362
where I graduated top of my year.

00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:09.463
However, I soon realized
there wasn't a place for me in London,

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:12.133
because whether you wish
to believe it or not,

00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:15.519
England is still a country
that is rooted and functions within

00:02:15.543 --> 00:02:16.704
the class system.

00:02:16.728 --> 00:02:22.671
And as a young, black, gay female artist
from a working-class family,

00:02:22.695 --> 00:02:24.425
I didn't stand a chance.

00:02:24.449 --> 00:02:27.283
So I left London and I moved to Japan,

00:02:27.307 --> 00:02:31.276
where I didn't experience
people asking me where I was really from.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.251
I was just another gaijin,

00:02:33.275 --> 00:02:36.206
which, ironically, means "outsider."

00:02:36.606 --> 00:02:40.669
I was immersed in a culture
that honors both making and craft,

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.879
where people perfect their craft
over generations.

00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:46.910
It's a culture that masters
both time and space,

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:49.826
so that artists can truly
create with freedom.

00:02:50.282 --> 00:02:53.813
And what I discovered
was a place I wasn't angry with.

00:02:54.155 --> 00:02:56.369
Tokyo hadn't wronged me in any way.

00:02:56.393 --> 00:02:58.903
I could no longer create with anger

00:02:58.927 --> 00:03:00.465
or out of pain.

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:04.253
I had to bravely allow myself
to create from a different place.

00:03:04.812 --> 00:03:07.693
And what I found is this incredible tool

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:10.672
transcended a line on paper.

00:03:10.696 --> 00:03:12.070
I found this thing

00:03:12.094 --> 00:03:14.807
that connected my head to my heart

00:03:14.831 --> 00:03:16.831
and my hand to everything.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.066
I could see the world in new ways.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:21.979
I found connections in corners

00:03:22.003 --> 00:03:24.966
and the solutions to problems
I never knew existed.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.252
It's like the world with all its
positive and negative spaces

00:03:28.276 --> 00:03:29.915
could now be seen.

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:31.371
And just by seeing it,

00:03:31.395 --> 00:03:33.283
there was no longer any fear.

00:03:33.307 --> 00:03:35.569
It's like my pen was a flashlight,

00:03:35.593 --> 00:03:38.053
and the unknown was still there,

00:03:38.077 --> 00:03:39.467
but it wasn't scary.

00:03:39.861 --> 00:03:43.926
After five years of living in Japan
and focusing on my craft,

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.400
I felt like I needed a new challenge.

00:03:46.903 --> 00:03:48.110
So I moved to New York,

00:03:48.134 --> 00:03:50.379
because that's what you do
as an artist, right?

00:03:50.403 --> 00:03:52.394
You move to the greatest
city in the world

00:03:52.418 --> 00:03:53.642
that has the ability

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:57.873
to make you feel
completely and utterly invisible.

00:03:58.788 --> 00:04:01.882
This is when I began to truly ask myself,

00:04:01.906 --> 00:04:04.255
"Who are you?"

00:04:04.279 --> 00:04:07.288
I would wake up in the morning,
and before I began my day,

00:04:07.312 --> 00:04:09.006
I would meditate on this.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.991
And with this question in mind,

00:04:11.015 --> 00:04:12.498
I kept drawing.

00:04:12.522 --> 00:04:14.124
I followed the line.

00:04:14.148 --> 00:04:15.791
I let it lead the way.

00:04:15.815 --> 00:04:17.995
The process of picking up a pen,

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.653
something everyone has access to,

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:24.077
the act of giving myself
permission to let go

00:04:24.101 --> 00:04:28.014
of all thoughts, all fears,
insecurities --

00:04:28.038 --> 00:04:29.688
anything that would get in the way

00:04:29.712 --> 00:04:32.490
of allowing myself to be completely me --

00:04:32.514 --> 00:04:35.726
that became my way
of experiencing freedom.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.361
When I got to New York,

00:04:37.385 --> 00:04:40.090
I didn't want to play by the rules
of the art world.

00:04:40.114 --> 00:04:43.288
I continued my practice as an outsider.

00:04:43.312 --> 00:04:44.796
I kept drawing.

00:04:45.249 --> 00:04:48.069
Curiosity became the ink for my pen,

00:04:48.093 --> 00:04:50.355
and I continued to dive deeper.

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:54.829
Over time, I began to create
a bold, confident space for myself,

00:04:54.853 --> 00:04:56.909
a space that was all my own.

00:04:56.933 --> 00:04:59.274
Initially, it was just my bedroom.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:02.320
But that bedroom ended up
in "The New York Times,"

00:05:02.344 --> 00:05:05.222
and suddenly, I was being seen and known

00:05:05.246 --> 00:05:07.246
for this world I had created.

00:05:08.034 --> 00:05:09.213
Since then,

00:05:09.237 --> 00:05:12.744
I've created and collaborated
with some of the most unique artists,

00:05:12.768 --> 00:05:14.655
institutions and spaces,

00:05:14.679 --> 00:05:16.855
from the screens of Times Square,

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:20.934
to the New York City Ballet
for their incredible artist series,

00:05:20.958 --> 00:05:23.434
where I interviewed a number of dancers.

00:05:23.458 --> 00:05:26.181
Their stories and words
became the foundation

00:05:26.205 --> 00:05:28.950
of over 30 drawings and artworks,

00:05:28.974 --> 00:05:31.268
which took over the promenade walls,

00:05:31.292 --> 00:05:33.467
windows and floors.

00:05:34.249 --> 00:05:35.495
For a long time,

00:05:35.519 --> 00:05:38.725
I wanted to create a space
for contemplation and poetry.

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:40.029
And in 2019,

00:05:40.053 --> 00:05:42.363
I was given the opportunity
to do just that

00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:44.198
by the Trust of Governor's Island.

00:05:44.585 --> 00:05:47.085
They provided me with the perfect canvas

00:05:47.109 --> 00:05:49.577
in the form of a former military chapel.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:51.349
Meet "The May Room."

00:05:51.792 --> 00:05:55.926
With drawings on the exterior
inspired by the history of the island,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.601
you walk inside, you take your shoes off,

00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:01.395
and there's a drawing on the floor
in the form of a maze

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:02.990
that brings you back to you.

00:06:03.014 --> 00:06:05.585
It's an invitation to become calm.

00:06:05.609 --> 00:06:09.222
And this allows you
to see phrases on the wall.

00:06:09.246 --> 00:06:10.968
"May you be wise."

00:06:10.992 --> 00:06:13.066
"May you sleep soundly at night."

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.275
"May we save trees."

00:06:15.299 --> 00:06:17.826
"May you," "may you," "may we."

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.934
And these phrases seem
like they're rising from you

00:06:20.958 --> 00:06:22.598
or falling into you.

00:06:24.074 --> 00:06:26.749
I've let my lines
become much like a language,

00:06:26.773 --> 00:06:29.471
a language that has unfolded
much like life.

00:06:29.495 --> 00:06:31.034
And when there has been silence,

00:06:31.058 --> 00:06:33.609
I've sought connection
through conversation,

00:06:33.633 --> 00:06:36.353
asking questions to push
through the discomfort.

00:06:36.377 --> 00:06:39.467
Drawing has taught me
how to create my own rules.

00:06:39.916 --> 00:06:43.822
It has taught me to open my eyes
to see not only what is,

00:06:43.846 --> 00:06:45.160
but what can be.

00:06:45.184 --> 00:06:47.715
And where there are broken systems,

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:52.656
we can create new ones
that actually function and benefit all,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.680
instead of just a select few.

00:06:55.046 --> 00:06:57.939
Drawing has taught me
how to fully engage with the world.

00:06:58.288 --> 00:07:01.820
And what I've come to realize
through this language of lines

00:07:01.844 --> 00:07:04.383
is not the importance of being seen,

00:07:04.407 --> 00:07:07.493
but rather the gift of seeing
that we give to others

00:07:07.517 --> 00:07:11.223
and how true freedom
is the ability to see.

00:07:11.247 --> 00:07:12.856
And I don't mean that literally,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.222
because sight is only one way
in which one can see.

00:07:16.246 --> 00:07:19.934
But what I mean is to experience
the world in its entirety,

00:07:19.958 --> 00:07:23.466
maybe even more so
during the most challenging moments

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.693
like the one we face today.

00:07:25.717 --> 00:07:27.096
I'm Shantell Martin.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.271
I draw.

00:07:28.295 --> 00:07:30.212
And I invite you to pick up a pen

00:07:30.236 --> 00:07:32.188
and see where it takes you.

00:07:32.212 --> 00:07:35.071
(Music)

